<text x="386.0" y="98" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.50s">• Why:</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. sre-sli-slo-D3 — </strong>What loses points versus what earns them in SLIs, SLOs, and Error Budgets. The left column is the interview anti-pattern; the right column is the strong answer.</figcaption></figure>

- **Incident** — a declared, time-boxed response to user-impacting degradation: saying the word 'incident' is what starts the machinery.
- **Incident Commander (IC)** — decides and coordinates; owns the incident, never the keyboard. The IC does not debug.
- **Communications lead** — owns status updates to stakeholders on a fixed cadence, in impact-first language.
- **Scribe** — owns the timeline: what happened, when, who did what. The timeline is the postmortem's raw material.
- **Severity** — the impact axis: how many users, how badly degraded. Separate from priority, which is the fix-ordering business decision.
- **Mitigation** — stopping the bleeding (rollback, shed load, fail over). Comes before understanding the cause.
- **Resolution** — the service is healthy and the incident is closed; the learning continues in the postmortem.
- **Blameless postmortem** — a review of systems and process, never people: timeline, impact, plural root causes, action items with owners and dates.
- **Paging policy** — which severities wake whom, and how fast. Severity without a paging policy is just vocabulary.

