<text x="386.0" y="98" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.50s">• Why:</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. sre-capacity-D3 — </strong>What loses points versus what earns them in Capacity Planning and Scaling. The left column is the interview anti-pattern; the right column is the strong answer.</figcaption></figure>

- **RPO (Recovery Point Objective)** — how much data loss is tolerable: bounds backup frequency.
- **RTO (Recovery Time Objective)** — how fast recovery must complete: bounds downtime.
- **Active-passive** — one region serves, one stands by: simpler failover, idle cost.
- **Active-active** — both regions serve: no idle cost, but needs conflict resolution for writes.
- **Backpressure** — when consumers lag producers, pressure must go somewhere: bounded queues that block or shed, so slowness propagates instead of memory growing until collapse.
- **Load shedding** — deliberately dropping low-priority work to protect the core: a choice, unlike uncontrolled collapse.
- **Cache stampede / thundering herd** — cold-start or hot-key-expiry floods that hit the origin at once; defenses include request coalescing and staggered TTLs.
- **Circuit breaker** — fails fast to a degraded path when a dependency is down, instead of piling up doomed requests.
- **Graceful degradation** — serving stale or reduced functionality during an outage: slightly old data beats no data.

