<text x="386.0" y="72" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.15s">• Correct answer:</text>
<text x="386.0" y="98" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.50s">• Why:</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. sre-mock-D3 — </strong>What loses points versus what earns them in SRE Mock Circuit. The left column is the interview anti-pattern; the right column is the strong answer.</figcaption></figure>

- **Trunk-based development** — small, frequent merges to main behind feature flags: branches are inventory, and this keeps inventory near zero.
- **CI stages** — lint → unit tests → build → integration tests → security scans → artifact publish: fast-first so failures surface in minutes, not at minute 39.
- **Immutable artifact** — build once, promote the same artifact through environments: what you tested is what you ship.
- **Registry** — where immutable artifacts live (container/image, package): makes 'what's running where' answerable.
- **Blue-green** — two full environments, instant cutover and rollback: costs double capacity.
- **Canary** — new version to a small percentage first, promoted on observed metrics: safest for risky changes.
- **Rolling** — gradual replacement of old with new: simplest, slowest rollback.
- **Pipeline-as-code** — the pipeline versioned, reviewed, and reproducible like the app: if it isn't in version control, it's folklore.
- **Rollback vs fix-forward** — the decision hinges on blast radius and diagnosis confidence: big blast or uncertain cause means roll back.

