<text x="386.0" y="98" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.50s">• Why:</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. devops-ci-cd-D3 — </strong>What loses points versus what earns them in CI/CD Systems. The left column is the interview anti-pattern; the right column is the strong answer.</figcaption></figure>

- **Declarative** — state the desired end state; the tool converges (Terraform, Kubernetes manifests): idempotent and auditable.
- **Imperative** — script the steps: flexible but order-dependent and drift-prone.
- **Plan / apply** — Terraform's loop: preview the diff, then converge. Plan-before-apply is non-negotiable.
- **State** — Terraform's record of reality: lives in a remote backend with locking, never on a laptop.
- **Module** — a reusable unit of infrastructure (one concern each: network, compute, data): modules are functions, environment roots are the programs that call them.
- **Drift** — reality diverging from declared config (the manual console click): detected by scheduled plan runs, prevented by making the console read-only.
- **Policy-as-code** — governance enforced at plan time: violations are blocked before they exist, because prevention beats detection.

