<text x="386.0" y="98" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.50s">• Why:</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. devops-mock-D3 — </strong>What loses points versus what earns them in DevOps Mock Circuit. The left column is the interview anti-pattern; the right column is the strong answer.</figcaption></figure>

- **Region** — a geographic area of cloud data centers: the unit of data residency and disaster recovery.
- **Availability Zone (AZ)** — isolated data center(s) within a region with independent power and networking: run across ≥2 AZs; one AZ is a single point of failure.
- **Edge** — points of presence near users for caching and termination: proximity, not resilience.
- **Shared responsibility** — the provider secures OF the cloud (physical, hypervisor, fabric); you secure what's IN it (data, IAM, OS, app config).
- **VM** — virtualized hardware with a guest OS: strongest isolation, heaviest footprint.
- **Container** — shared host kernel, packaged userland: lighter and faster than VMs, weaker isolation.
- **Serverless** — functions the provider scales to zero: no servers to manage, pay per invocation, mind the cold starts.
- **CapEx** — buy servers upfront: fixed, depreciating. **OpEx** — pay per use: variable, agile, and in need of FinOps governance.
- **Vendor-neutral knowledge** — concepts that transfer (VPCs, IAM, queues, object storage) vs vendor-specific click-paths that expire with the UI.

