<text x="386.0" y="98" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.50s">• Why:</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. sec-appsec-D3 — </strong>What loses points versus what earns them in Application Security Fundamentals. The left column is the interview anti-pattern; the right column is the strong answer.</figcaption></figure>

- **TLS handshake** — ClientHello → ServerHello + certificate → key exchange (ECDHE for forward secrecy) → encrypted session. Validates: chain to a trusted root, hostname match, expiry.
- **Forward secrecy** — compromise of long-term keys doesn't decrypt past sessions: the ECDHE property.
- **Certificate pinning** — clients accept only specific certs/keys: for high-value mobile/API clients.
- **Segmentation** — defense in depth through zones (DMZ, app tier, data tier): traffic flows only where explicitly allowed, east-west as well as north-south.
- **Zero trust** — 'never trust, always verify': identity-based access, least privilege, device posture, continuous verification, micro-segmentation.
- **DNSSEC / DoH / DoT** — integrity for responses (signed), privacy for queries (encrypted transport).
- **DNS tunneling** — data encoded in DNS queries to an attacker domain: DNS is rarely filtered, so it becomes a covert channel.
- **Volumetric vs L7 DDoS** — bandwidth floods vs expensive legitimate-looking requests: absorbed at the edge vs filtered by behavioral/WAF rules.

