<text x="386.0" y="98" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.50s">• Why:</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. sec-network-D3 — </strong>What loses points versus what earns them in Network Security in Practice. The left column is the interview anti-pattern; the right column is the strong answer.</figcaption></figure>

- **Symmetric crypto (AES)** — one shared key, fast: for bulk data. **Asymmetric (RSA/ECDH)** — key pairs, slow: for key exchange and signatures. Hybrid in practice: asymmetric establishes, symmetric encrypts.
- **Hashing vs password storage** — fast hashes (MD5/SHA-256) are for integrity, never passwords: GPUs crack them. Passwords get Argon2id (or bcrypt) with unique per-user salts and a tuned work factor.
- **Salt / pepper** — unique per-user salt defeats rainbow tables; a pepper via KMS/HSM adds defense in depth.
- **Forward secrecy** — ECDHE key exchange: past sessions stay secret even if long-term keys leak.
- **KMS/HSM** — keys live in managed hardware/services, never in code or repos; envelope encryption; rotation on schedule and on incident.
- **CSPRNG** — cryptographic randomness (/dev/urandom, secrets module) for keys, tokens, nonces: predictable randomness is no randomness.
- **The never-roll-your-own rule** — subtle flaws survive testing and review; even experts get it wrong. Use vetted primitives.

