<text x="386.0" y="98" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="12" fill="#f0f6fc" text-anchor="start" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:3.50s">• Why:</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. sec-crypto-D3 — </strong>What loses points versus what earns them in Cryptography in Practice. The left column is the interview anti-pattern; the right column is the strong answer.</figcaption></figure>

- **MITRE ATT&CK** — the shared map of adversary behavior: tactics (why — initial access, persistence, exfiltration) → techniques (how).
- **Detection engineering** — detections mapped to ATT&CK techniques, built on verified log sources, tuned for precision/recall, tested with simulated adversary activity.
- **Log sources** — the telemetry that matters: auth logs, process execution, network flows, DNS, cloud audit trails — each verified to produce what the detection needs.
- **Precision vs recall** — precision: alerts that fire are real. Recall: real attacks fire alerts. Untuned rules destroy SOC trust.
- **NIST 800-61 phases** — Preparation → Detection & Analysis → Containment → Eradication → Recovery → Lessons Learned.
- **Contain before eradicate** — stop the bleeding and preserve evidence before removing the threat; eradicating first can tip off the adversary and destroy forensics.

